# 1. Rectangle — Classes, Objects, Constructors, Getters, Setters, `this`

```java
public class RectangleExample {

    // main() is where Java starts running the program
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Creates a new Rectangle object.
        //
        // Rectangle = the class/type
        // r1 = reference variable that refers to the Rectangle object
        // new = tells Java to create a new object
        // Rectangle(5, 3) = calls the constructor
        // 5 = argument for length
        // 3 = argument for width
        Rectangle r1 = new Rectangle(5, 3);


        // Calls the getLength() method on r1.
        // getLength() returns the value stored in the length data field.
        System.out.println("Length: " + r1.getLength());


        // Calls the getWidth() method on r1.
        System.out.println("Width: " + r1.getWidth());


        // Calls getArea().
        // The method calculates length * width and returns the result.
        System.out.println("Area: " + r1.getArea());


        // Calls getPerimeter().
        // The method calculates 2 * (length + width).
        System.out.println("Perimeter: " + r1.getPerimeter());


        // Calls the setter and changes r1's length from 5 to 10.
        //
        // The 10 gets passed into the parameter:
        // setLength(double length)
        r1.setLength(10);


        // Since we changed the length to 10,
        // the area is now:
        //
        // 10 * 3 = 30
        System.out.println("New Area: " + r1.getArea());
    }
}


// This is the Rectangle class.
//
// A class is a BLUEPRINT for creating objects.
//
// Every Rectangle object created from this class
// can have its own length and width.
class Rectangle {

    // DATA FIELDS
    //
    // These variables store information about each Rectangle object.
    //
    // private means these variables should only be accessed
    // directly from inside the Rectangle class.
    private double length;
    private double width;


    // NO-ARGUMENT CONSTRUCTOR
    //
    // A constructor has:
    // 1. The SAME name as the class
    // 2. NO return type, not even void
    //
    // This constructor runs when we write:
    //
    // Rectangle r = new Rectangle();
    public Rectangle() {

        // Give the Rectangle default values.
        length = 1;
        width = 1;
    }


    // CONSTRUCTOR WITH PARAMETERS
    //
    // This constructor allows us to provide a length and width
    // when creating the object.
    //
    // Example:
    //
    // Rectangle r = new Rectangle(5, 3);
    //
    // length parameter receives 5
    // width parameter receives 3
    public Rectangle(double length, double width) {

        // this.length means:
        // "the length data field belonging to THIS object"
        //
        // length by itself means:
        // "the parameter that was passed into this constructor"
        //
        // So if we called:
        //
        // new Rectangle(5, 3)
        //
        // this.length becomes 5.
        this.length = length;

        // Same idea:
        //
        // object's width = width parameter
        this.width = width;
    }


    // GETTER METHOD
    //
    // A getter allows code outside the class to READ
    // a private data field.
    //
    // double means this method RETURNS a double.
    public double getLength() {

        // Return the object's current length.
        return length;
    }


    // SETTER METHOD
    //
    // A setter allows code outside the class to CHANGE
    // a private data field.
    //
    // void means the method does NOT return anything.
    public void setLength(double length) {

        // Change this object's length to the value
        // passed into the method.
        this.length = length;
    }


    // Getter for width.
    public double getWidth() {
        return width;
    }


    // Setter for width.
    public void setWidth(double width) {
        this.width = width;
    }


    // Calculates and RETURNS the area.
    public double getArea() {

        // Area of rectangle:
        //
        // length * width
        return length * width;
    }


    // Calculates and RETURNS the perimeter.
    public double getPerimeter() {

        // Perimeter:
        //
        // 2(length + width)
        return 2 * (length + width);
    }
}
```

