# 2. Rational Class — VERY IMPORTANT

```java
public class RationalExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Create a Rational object representing:
        //
        // 5
        // -
        // 2
        //
        // r1 is the reference variable.
        Rational r1 = new Rational(5, 2);


        // Create another Rational object representing:
        //
        // 7
        // -
        // 3
        Rational r2 = new Rational(7, 3);


        // Call the add() method using r1.
        //
        // VERY IMPORTANT:
        //
        // r1.add(r2)
        //
        // Inside the add() method:
        //
        // this = r1
        // r    = r2
        //
        // The add() method creates and returns
        // a NEW Rational object.
        Rational r3 = r1.add(r2);


        // Because Rational has a toString() method,
        // Java knows how we want a Rational object displayed.
        System.out.println("r1 = " + r1);
        System.out.println("r2 = " + r2);
        System.out.println("r3 = " + r3);
    }
}


class Rational {

    // Each Rational object stores:
    //
    // numerator
    // -----------
    // denominator
    //
    // Example:
    //
    // 5/2
    //
    // numerator = 5
    // denominator = 2
    private int numerator;
    private int denominator;


    // Constructor.
    //
    // Example:
    //
    // new Rational(5, 2)
    //
    // numerator parameter receives 5
    // denominator parameter receives 2
    public Rational(int numerator, int denominator) {

        // Store the numerator parameter
        // inside THIS Rational object.
        this.numerator = numerator;

        // Store the denominator parameter
        // inside THIS Rational object.
        this.denominator = denominator;
    }


    // Getter for numerator.
    public int getNumerator() {
        return numerator;
    }


    // Getter for denominator.
    public int getDenominator() {
        return denominator;
    }


    // This method adds THIS Rational object
    // to another Rational object called r.
    //
    // Notice the return type is Rational.
    //
    // That means this method RETURNS
    // a Rational object.
    public Rational add(Rational r) {

        // Imagine we called:
        //
        // r1.add(r2)
        //
        // where:
        //
        // r1 = 5/2
        // r2 = 7/3
        //
        // Inside THIS method:
        //
        // this = r1 = 5/2
        // r    = r2 = 7/3


        // Formula for adding fractions:
        //
        // a/b + c/d
        //
        // numerator:
        //
        // (a * d) + (b * c)
        //
        // For:
        //
        // 5/2 + 7/3
        //
        // (5 * 3) + (2 * 7)
        //
        // 15 + 14
        //
        // = 29
        int n =
            this.numerator * r.denominator
            +
            this.denominator * r.numerator;


        // New denominator:
        //
        // b * d
        //
        // 2 * 3
        //
        // = 6
        int d =
            this.denominator * r.denominator;


        // Create a NEW Rational object.
        //
        // n = 29
        // d = 6
        //
        // So this returns:
        //
        // new Rational(29, 6)
        return new Rational(n, d);
    }


    // toString() determines how the object
    // should look when converted to a String.
    //
    // Without our own toString(), printing an object
    // may display something that looks like:
    //
    // Rational@6d06d69c
    //
    // Instead, we want:
    //
    // 5/2
    public String toString() {

        return numerator + "/" + denominator;
    }
}
```

### Rational Shortcut

```text
r1.add(r2)

this = r1
r    = r2
```

If:

```text
r1 = 5/2
r2 = 7/3
```

then:

```text
this.numerator   = 5
this.denominator = 2

r.numerator      = 7
r.denominator    = 3
```

