# 4. `super` — Calling the Parent Constructor

```java
public class SuperExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Create a Rectangle.
        Rectangle r1 = new Rectangle("Blue", 5, 10);

        System.out.println("Color: " + r1.getColor());
        System.out.println("Area: " + r1.getArea());
    }
}


class GeometricObject {

    private String color;


    // Parent/superclass constructor.
    public GeometricObject(String color) {

        // Store the color in the GeometricObject part
        // of the object.
        this.color = color;
    }


    public String getColor() {
        return color;
    }
}


class Rectangle extends GeometricObject {

    private double width;
    private double height;


    public Rectangle(
        String color,
        double width,
        double height
    ) {

        // super refers to the SUPERCLASS.
        //
        // Rectangle's superclass is GeometricObject.
        //
        // Therefore:
        //
        // super(color)
        //
        // calls:
        //
        // GeometricObject(color)
        //
        // If color = "Blue", this essentially calls:
        //
        // GeometricObject("Blue")
        //
        // A call to super(...) must be the first
        // statement in the constructor.
        super(color);


        // These fields belong to Rectangle.
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }


    public double getArea() {
        return width * height;
    }
}
```

### Remember

```text
this  → current object

super → superclass / parent
```

