# 5. Abstract Class

```java
public class AbstractExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // We CAN create a Circle because Circle
        // is not abstract.
        Circle c1 = new Circle(5);


        // We CAN create a Rectangle because
        // Rectangle is not abstract.
        Rectangle r1 = new Rectangle(4, 6);


        System.out.println(
            "Circle area: " + c1.getArea()
        );


        System.out.println(
            "Rectangle area: " + r1.getArea()
        );


        // THIS WOULD NOT WORK:
        //
        // GeometricObject g =
        //     new GeometricObject();
        //
        // Why?
        //
        // Because GeometricObject is ABSTRACT.
        // An abstract class cannot be directly instantiated.
    }
}


// "abstract" means this class is designed
// to serve as a superclass.
//
// Other classes can EXTEND it.
abstract class GeometricObject {

    private String color;


    public void setColor(String color) {
        this.color = color;
    }


    public String getColor() {
        return color;
    }


    // ABSTRACT METHOD
    //
    // Notice:
    //
    // 1. It uses the word abstract
    // 2. It has NO method body { }
    // 3. It ends with ;
    //
    // GeometricObject is basically saying:
    //
    // "Every concrete subclass must know
    // how to calculate its area."
    //
    // But GeometricObject itself does not know
    // the formula because different shapes
    // have different area formulas.
    public abstract double getArea();
}


class Circle extends GeometricObject {

    private double radius;


    public Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }


    // Circle provides its own implementation
    // of the inherited abstract getArea() method.
    public double getArea() {

        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}


class Rectangle extends GeometricObject {

    private double width;
    private double height;


    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {

        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }


    // Rectangle provides its own version
    // of getArea().
    public double getArea() {

        return width * height;
    }
}
```

### Abstract Class Rule

```java
abstract class GeometricObject
```

You CANNOT do:

```java
new GeometricObject();
```

But you CAN do:

```java
new Circle(5);
new Rectangle(4, 6);
```

