# 7. ComparableRectangle — EXTREMELY IMPORTANT

```java
public class ComparableExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Create the first rectangle.
        //
        // width = 5
        // height = 10
        //
        // area = 50
        ComparableRectangle r1 =
            new ComparableRectangle(5, 10);


        // Create second rectangle.
        //
        // width = 2
        // height = 4
        //
        // area = 8
        ComparableRectangle r2 =
            new ComparableRectangle(2, 4);


        // Compare r1 with r2.
        //
        // Since we are calling:
        //
        // r1.compareTo(r2)
        //
        // inside compareTo():
        //
        // this = r1
        // r    = r2
        //
        // r1 area = 50
        // r2 area = 8
        //
        // Since 50 > 8,
        // compareTo() returns a positive number.
        int result = r1.compareTo(r2);


        // Positive means r1 is considered larger.
        if (result > 0) {

            System.out.println("r1 is larger");
        }


        // Negative means r1 is considered smaller.
        else if (result < 0) {

            System.out.println("r1 is smaller");
        }


        // Zero means they are considered equal.
        else {

            System.out.println("They are equal");
        }
    }
}


// Regular Rectangle class.
class Rectangle {

    private double width;
    private double height;


    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {

        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }


    public double getArea() {

        return width * height;
    }
}


// This class does TWO important things:
//
// 1. extends Rectangle
//
//    This means ComparableRectangle IS-A Rectangle
//    and inherits accessible Rectangle methods.
//
// 2. implements Comparable<ComparableRectangle>
//
//    This means ComparableRectangle objects
//    can be compared with each other.
class ComparableRectangle
    extends Rectangle
    implements Comparable<ComparableRectangle> {


    public ComparableRectangle(
        double width,
        double height
    ) {

        // ComparableRectangle inherited from Rectangle.
        //
        // Rectangle has this constructor:
        //
        // Rectangle(double width, double height)
        //
        // super(width, height) calls that
        // parent constructor.
        super(width, height);
    }


    // Comparable requires us to provide
    // the compareTo() method.
    //
    // We are deciding to compare Rectangles
    // based on AREA.
    public int compareTo(ComparableRectangle r) {


        // this.getArea()
        //
        // means:
        //
        // get the area of the object
        // that called compareTo().
        //
        // Example:
        //
        // r1.compareTo(r2)
        //
        // this.getArea() = r1's area
        //
        // r.getArea() = r2's area
        if (this.getArea() > r.getArea()) {

            // Positive means THIS object is larger.
            return 1;
        }


        else if (this.getArea() < r.getArea()) {

            // Negative means THIS object is smaller.
            return -1;
        }


        else {

            // Zero means they are considered equal.
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

### `compareTo()` Cheat Sheet

```text
r1.compareTo(r2)

this = r1
r    = r2


return negative
→ r1 is smaller


return 0
→ r1 and r2 are equal


return positive
→ r1 is larger
```

